# =============================================================================
# ~/.aliases  -  Shared aliases for bash / zsh / fish / nushell / PowerShell,
#                read by the single-file `config.{ext}` loaders.
#
# Copy this file to ~/.aliases and edit it to your taste:
#
#     cp aliases.example ~/.aliases
#
# Missing file is silently ignored.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FORMAT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   name=command           # alias `name` runs `command`
#   name='cmd --flag x'    # single quotes are stripped
#   name="cmd --flag x"    # double quotes are stripped
#   # comment              # full-line comments start with `#`
#   <blank line>           ignored
#
# Rules:
#   * One alias per line. No trailing `;`, no continuations.
#   * `name` must match [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_-]*.
#   * The FIRST `=` separates name and command. Subsequent `=` stay in command.
#   * Inline `#` after a value is NOT a comment.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARD RULES — what may live here
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An alias belongs here only if ALL of these hold:
#
#   1. SINGLE EXTERNAL COMMAND + literal args. The body must start with a
#      real executable (git, docker, npm, …). No pipelines `|`, no
#      `&&`/`||`, no `$()`/backticks, no redirects, no `$VAR` expansion,
#      no shell builtins.
#
#   2. Neither the alias NAME nor the FIRST WORD of the body collides with
#      a built-in/auto-alias in nushell or PowerShell. This is the hard
#      constraint that makes the file 5-shell-safe.
#
#      nushell built-in names to avoid:
#         cd clear cp date du exit find history kill ls mkdir mv open
#         port ps rm sleep which
#      PowerShell auto-alias names to avoid (a sample of the common ones):
#         cd cls copy del dir echo erase gc gcm gci gi gl gm gp gps gv h
#         history md mi move ni r ren ri rm select set sl sv type where
#
#   3. Flags work on every target OS (Linux, macOS, BSD, Windows). `df -h`
#      is OK; `ps -ef` is not (BSD differs).
#
# Anything that fails a rule should be written directly in your shell rc
# (where you can use that shell's native syntax).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NUSHELL CAVEAT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# nushell parses `alias` at parse time, so it cannot read this text file
# and install aliases at startup. The nushell loader (config.nu) handles
# this by translating ~/.aliases into a cached file the FIRST time you
# source it. After editing ~/.aliases, run `shells-regen-aliases` from
# inside nushell to refresh the cache, then re-source config.nu.
#
# =============================================================================

# ---- Example entries; uncomment and edit as needed --------------------------

# df=df -h
# now=date +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z
# g=git
# gst=git status
# gpsu=git push -u origin HEAD
